    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The fabricator said seal it every year. The internet says granite is porous. Your slab, meanwhile, has an opinion of its own, and it is the only one that counts.
        </p>

        <div id="why" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why One Granite Needs It and Another Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            Granite is a natural stone, not a manufactured product, and its porosity varies enormously between slabs quarried a hundred miles apart. Dense, dark stones such as Absolute Black and Uba Tuba are frequently so tight that a sealer cannot get in at all. Light, heavily patterned stones with large mineral grains — many whites, Kashmir and Colonial varieties — can be genuinely absorbent and stain within minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            The colour correlation is real but not reliable enough to act on alone. What actually governs absorption is the mineral composition and how tightly the crystals interlock, and two slabs of the same commercial name from different quarry blocks can behave differently. This is why no honest answer to whether granite needs sealing exists without testing the specific countertop in question.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish matters too. A polished surface has been ground and buffed until the pore openings at the top face are substantially closed, so the same stone in a honed or leathered finish will absorb noticeably faster. If you have a polished island and a honed perimeter of the same granite, test them separately — they can genuinely give different answers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fabricators frequently seal countertops before installation, and some apply a factory treatment at the slab yard as well. That treatment is real but finite, and it is the reason a new countertop can pass the water test comfortably for two years and then start failing it. Testing annually is the only way to catch that transition when it happens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Water Test, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the standard test, it takes fifteen minutes, and it needs nothing you do not already own. Clean the area first with a stone-safe cleaner or plain warm water, and let it dry completely — a residue of cooking oil or polish will skew the result towards making the stone look better protected than it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pour about a quarter cup of water onto a flat area of the countertop, ideally in a spot that gets real use rather than a corner behind the kettle. Let it sit for fifteen minutes. Set a timer rather than guessing, because the difference between ten minutes and twenty changes how you should read the result.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Quarter cup of water, fifteen minutes, then wipe and look.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A dark patch that fades within a minute or two of wiping means the stone took on water and needs sealing. No mark at all means it did not, and sealing would achieve nothing. Anything in between means test again in six months.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Wipe the water away with a dry cloth and look at the stone immediately, then again after a minute. A darkened ring or patch that remains is water inside the surface of the stone. It is not damage and it will evaporate out over the next ten to thirty minutes, but it is the evidence you were looking for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repeat in at least three places: beside the sink, next to the cooktop, and on the island or a low-traffic run. Sealer wears fastest where surfaces are cleaned most and where hot pans and oil land, so a countertop that passes on the island can easily fail beside the hob. Test the areas you actually use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Reading the Result Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            The result is a spectrum rather than a pass or fail, and the time it takes for a mark to appear tells you how urgent the job is. The table below is how we interpret it, and it maps directly onto what we would do next in each case.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What Happens</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Means</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Action</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dark mark within 2-4 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very absorbent, unsealed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seal now, expect 2 coats</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dark mark at 10-15 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealer worn or weak</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seal this month</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Faint mark, fades fast</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealer wearing thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Retest in 6 months</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">No mark at 15 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealed or naturally dense</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Retest in 12 months</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">No mark at 30 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Effectively non-porous</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No sealing needed</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The most common misreading is treating a faint, quickly fading shadow as a failure. Almost any stone will show some momentary darkening under standing water, particularly around a seam or a fissure, and sealing on that basis puts product onto a surface that cannot absorb it. The excess then dries on top as a hazy film, which is a real problem created in pursuit of an imaginary one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other misreading runs the opposite way: testing only the pristine back corner, getting a clean result, and concluding the whole countertop is fine. The area beside the sink has been cleaned five thousand times more often than that corner, and cleaning is what removes an impregnating sealer. Test where the work happens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oil" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Oil Test, and Why It Matters More
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water is the convenient test, but oil is the stain that actually ruins countertops. Olive oil, butter and the fat spitting out of a pan are the everyday hazards in a kitchen, and oil penetrates stone that water cannot. A granite that passes the water test can still take an oil stain, which is why a second test is worth doing before you conclude that sealing is unnecessary.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put a few drops of cooking oil on an inconspicuous area — inside the sink cutout edge or under the overhang works well — and leave it for thirty minutes. Wipe it off and look. A darkened patch that does not fade within an hour means the oil went into the stone, and that is the situation an impregnating sealer exists to prevent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not run this test in the middle of the countertop the first time. An oil mark on absorbent granite can take days to draw out fully, and on a light stone it may need a poultice to clear. Our guide to <a href='/how-to-remove-stains-from-granite/'>removing stains from granite</a> covers the poultice method if you get an answer you did not want in a place you can see.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the water test passes and the oil test fails, seal the countertop. That combination is common on mid-density granites, and it is the specific case where people conclude their stone is fine and then find a permanent shadow under the olive oil bottle six months later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Signs You Have Already Waited Too Long
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before testing, look at the countertop in raking light from the side. A slab that needs sealing urgently usually says so: darker patches around the tap and the cooktop, a ring under the soap dispenser, and a general dullness in the areas that get wet most often. Those are not dirt, and cleaning harder will not remove them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water rings that reappear after every wipe are a reliable sign. Once the surface is open, every spill wets the stone rather than sitting on it, and the marks take longer to evaporate each time. A countertop that looks permanently damp beside the sink has lost whatever protection it had some time ago.
          </p>

          <p>
            Absorbed stains are the next stage and they are the point at which sealing stops being the answer. A sealer applied over an existing stain locks it in. The stain has to come out first, which on granite usually means a poultice and patience, and only then is there any point in sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Etching is a separate problem people often confuse with staining, and it matters because sealing does not prevent it. An etch is a dull spot where acid has chemically attacked the stone, leaving the surface physically changed. Granite is far more acid-resistant than marble, so etching is rare on it — but if you have dull spots where lemon juice landed, our <a href='/best-marble-sealer/'>marble sealer guide</a> explains the mechanism, because it is a marble problem that occasionally appears on softer granites.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="never" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Granite That Never Needs Sealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            A meaningful share of granite countertops genuinely do not need sealing, ever. Dense black stones, many of the deep greens, and several of the harder browns are tight enough that an impregnating sealer cannot enter the stone at all. Applying one to them wastes product and risks leaving a residue on the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tell is the thirty-minute water test. If standing water for half an hour leaves no mark at all, there is nowhere for a sealer to go. Some of these stones are sold with an assurance that they never need sealing, and for once the marketing is accurate — but test rather than take it on faith, because commercial stone names cover a lot of variation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Engineered quartz is the other case, and it comes up constantly because people cannot always tell the two apart. Quartz surfaces are roughly 90 percent ground stone bound in resin, and the resin is non-porous. They never need sealing, and sealer applied to them sits on the surface and hazes. If your countertop has an unnaturally consistent pattern with no visible mineral crystals or fissures, check whether it is quartz before buying anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing a stone that does not need it is not catastrophic, but it is not harmless either. Excess impregnator that cannot soak in dries as a patchy, slightly sticky film that attracts dirt and is awkward to remove, usually requiring the same solvent the sealer was carried in. The test exists precisely to avoid that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Do With the Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the stone failed at two to four minutes, it is thirsty and it needs a proper impregnating sealer applied in two coats. Expect it to take a lot of product on the first pass and considerably less on the second. The method is covered step by step in our guide to <a href='/how-to-seal-granite-countertops/'>sealing granite countertops</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If it failed at ten to fifteen minutes, one coat will usually restore it. This is the most common result on a countertop that was sealed at installation two or three years ago, and catching it here rather than a year later is the difference between a maintenance coat and a stain removal project.
          </p>

          <p>
            If it passed, write the date somewhere and test again in twelve months. The transition from passing to failing is gradual and invisible day to day, and an annual test on the same three spots turns it into something you notice early. Our guide to <a href='/how-often-to-seal-granite-countertops/'>how often to seal granite</a> sets out a realistic schedule by stone type.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the result, choose the product before the weekend rather than on the way home. Impregnating sealers for food-contact surfaces are not all equivalent, and the difference between a solvent-carried fluoropolymer and a cheap water-based silicone shows up in how long the next test takes to fail. Our <a href='/best-granite-sealer/'>granite sealer picks</a> cover what we have found holds up on real kitchens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing Only the Clean Corner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The back corner behind the kettle has been cleaned a fraction as often as the run beside the sink, and cleaning is what wears an impregnating sealer away. Test at least three areas including the ones that get worked hardest, or you are testing the wrong countertop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading a Momentary Shadow as Failure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almost any stone darkens slightly under standing water, especially near fissures. A faint mark that fades within a minute of wiping is not a failure. Sealing on that basis leaves excess product drying on a surface that could not absorb it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Oil Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil penetrates stone that water cannot, and oil is what actually stains kitchen countertops. A granite that passes on water and fails on oil is common, and it is exactly the case where people decide sealing is unnecessary and regret it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over an Existing Stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An impregnating sealer applied over an absorbed stain locks it into the stone permanently. Draw the stain out with a poultice first, let the area dry fully, and only then seal. The order is not negotiable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Quartz</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Engineered quartz is bound in resin and is non-porous, so sealer cannot enter it and simply hazes on the surface. If the pattern is unnaturally uniform with no real mineral crystals or fissures, confirm what the countertop is before buying anything.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my granite countertop needs sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pour a quarter cup of water on a clean, dry, well-used area and leave it fifteen minutes. Wipe it off: a dark patch that lingers means the stone absorbed water and needs sealing. No mark means it did not. Test at least three spots, because the area beside the sink wears far faster than the island.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does all granite need to be sealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Dense stones such as Absolute Black and many deep greens are tight enough that a sealer cannot penetrate at all, and applying one leaves a hazy residue. Light, coarse-grained granites are often genuinely absorbent. Only the water and oil tests on your specific slab can tell you which you have.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should water sit on granite to test it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fifteen minutes is the standard. If you want to know whether the stone is effectively non-porous, extend it to thirty — a slab that shows nothing after half an hour does not need sealing at all. Timing it matters, because how quickly a mark appears tells you how urgent the job is.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will water stain granite permanently?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Water darkens granite temporarily by filling the pores, and it evaporates out over ten to thirty minutes leaving no mark. What does stain permanently is oil, and to a lesser extent wine, coffee and strongly coloured foods, which is why the oil test matters more than the water one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I test granite with oil instead of water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and you should do both. A few drops of cooking oil left for thirty minutes on an inconspicuous area — under the overhang or inside the sink cutout — will reveal absorbency that water misses. Do not run it in the middle of the countertop first, because an oil mark on thirsty stone can take days to clear.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I test my granite?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Annually, on the same three spots, and always after any resurfacing or deep cleaning work. A countertop moves from passing to failing gradually and invisibly, and an annual test turns that into something you catch while a single maintenance coat still fixes it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing granite make it stain-proof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, it makes it stain-resistant. An impregnating sealer slows absorption, buying you minutes to hours to wipe up a spill rather than seconds. Oil, wine and turmeric left overnight on sealed granite can still mark it. The sealer buys time, not immunity.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

